The successful preparation of polyMOFs from bpy and pbdc-xa inspired us to explore whether other ‘pillaring’ linkers, such as dabco and 1,2-bis $\overline{{4}}$ -pyridyl)ethane (bpe), could also form polyMOFs.  Following reported procedures, reactions of dabco, pbdc- $x\mathbf{a}$ , and $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ in a 1:2:2 stoichiometry did not afford the expected MOF products, $\left([\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{\mathrm{n}}\right)$ , instead forming IRMOF materials (which do not incorporate the pillar) or amorphous materials (Figure S16).  This result can be ascribed to the spatial match limitation in the $\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ structure. An examination of the $\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{bdc}})_{2}(\ensuremath{\mathbf{dabco}})\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ structure found the nominal distance between the adjacent bdc moieties is ${\sim}7{\cdot}4$ Å (carbon-carbon distance, Figure $\mathrm{S_{17}}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ).  However, among all of the pbdc-xa $\left(x=5\mathbf{-}12\right)$ ligands prepared, the shortest bdc distance is ${\sim}7.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for pbdc-5a, which is longer than the bdcbdc distance in $\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ .  This suggests a size mismatch can account for the inability of these polyMOF analogues to form.
The apparent length mismatch prompted us to try a longer co-ligand linker, bpe, which might better accommodate the alkane spacers in pbdc-xa.  Reactions of bpe, pbdc-xa $x=7^{-}$ 12), and $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ with a 1:2:2 stoichiometry in DMF at 120 $^{\circ}C$ afforded light yellow powders.  PXRD patterns revealed that all products exhibited an unknown crystalline phase (Figure 4).  The morphology and particle size of the resulting materials were determined by SEM (Figure 5).  As shown in Figure 4, the majority of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -pbdc- $.{x}\mathbf{a}$ (bpe) particles possess a hexagonal shape on the order of $<5\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ , indicating a possible hexagonal crystallization space group.
Because the small Zn-pbdc-xa(bpe) particles were not suitable for single crystal $\mathrm{x}$ -ray diffraction (XRD), we replaced the polymer ligands with a simple $\scriptstyle\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}$ ligand to generate an analogous MOF framework (essentially the reverse process used to design the Zn-pbdc-xa(bpy) derivatives, Figure 1).  Under the guideline of this strategy, BA-bdc (Chart 1) was employed to grow MOF crystals under the same synthesis condition as Zn-pbdc-xa(bpe).  Brown block crystals with a formula of $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{7}(b d c)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{6}(b p e)_{2}(N O_{3})_{2}]_{n}}}$ (2) were harvested.  XRD analysis showed that MOF 2 crystallized in the hexagonal $R\mathrm{.}$ - $_{3}m$ space group.  As shown in Figure 6a, MOF 2 exhibits two kinds of SBUs, a $^4$ -connected $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(C O O)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{3}N]}}$ dinuclear SBU and an 8-connected $[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{COO})_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}]$ trinuclear SBU. These two nodes are connected by BA-bdc ligands to form a 2-dimensional rhombic-grid layer that is further cross-linked by bpe pillars to generate a $^{4,8}$ -connected fluorite $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{flu})$ network.24  Figure 6b shows large free pore spaces in the MOF $\scriptstyle2$ structure.  As shown in Figure 5, the experimental PXRD pattern of Zn-pbdc- $.x\mathbf{a}$ $\left(x=7\mathbf{-}\mathbf{1}2\right)$ fit very well with the calculated pattern of MOF 2 except for the first two peaks are shifted slightly to higher 2θ values.  This may be due to slight differences in the unit cells between the polyMOFs and MOF 2, which could be accommodated as both the polymer ligand and bpe linker are quite flexible.
To further validate our design methodology, we screened reaction conditions of BA-bdc with ‘pillaring’ pyridine linkers and different metal salts.  Using $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2},$ a noninterpenetrated, pillared, squared grid MOF $\mathbf{3}\mathrm{{:}}$ , $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(B A-}$ bdc) ${\bf\Gamma}_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]_{\mathrm{n}},$ was obtained.23  The same reaction conditions using pbdc-8a produced an analogous polyMOF, Cu-pbdc$8\mathsf{a}(\mathsf{b p y})$ , which shows the same structure as the parent $\mathrm{\left[Cu_{2}(B A\mathrm{-}b d c)_{2}(b p y)\right]_{n}}$ .  Both SEM and PXRD data verified the high crystallinity of Cu-pbdc-8a(bpy) (Figure S18, S19). $\Nu_{2}$ sorption data revealed that Cu-bpdc-8a(bpy) is permanently porous and exhibits a BET surface area of $303{\pm}32\ \mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure S20).
To exclude the possibility of polymer ligand degradation during the polyMOF synthesis process, as-synthesized polyMOFs (based on both bpy or bpe) were digested in concentrated aqueous $\mathrm{D}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{DCl}$ and then precipitated by adding excess water. $\mathrm{^{1}H\ N M R}$ data confirmed that the recovered polymer ligands remained intact (Figure S21-S29). $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR data showed that the ratio of polymer ligand to bpy or bpe was greater than that expected from the known stoichiometry of MOF 1 or 2. $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-8a(bpy), - 9a(bpy), and -12a(bpy) showed that ratios of pbdc-xa (based on the number of bdc units) to bpy are \~3:1, ${\sim}43\$ and ${\sim}23\$ respectively, while the ratio from the structure of MOF 1 (i.e. based on $\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{BME\mathrm{-}b d c})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ and MOF 1') should be 2:1. $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR of Zn-pbdc- $.x\mathbf{a}$ (bpe) $\mathbf{\Psi}(x\ =\ 7–12)$ ) showed a ratio of pbdc-xa (based on the number of bdc units) to bpe ranging from ${\sim}43$ to $5^{\cdot1}$ , while the ratio based on the composition of MOF 2 should be 3:1 $(\mathrm{[Zn_{7}(B A\mathrm{-}b d c)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{6}(b p e)_{2}(N O_{3})_{2}]_{n}})$ . One possible reason for the apparent excess of polymer in the polyMOFs is that the polymer ligand may create structural defects in the polyMOFs, where the bpy and bpe ligands are absent from some SBUs.  Another possiblity is perhaps that not all bdc groups in pbdc-xa ligands can participate in framework formation, resulting in some bdc2- groups from the pbdc-xa ligands not being coordinated to the SBUs (i.e. dangling bdc2- ligands within the polyMOFs). It is also possible that polymer ligands extend outside polyMOF crystal domains, serving as coating or binder on the particle surfaces; which perhaps explains the polyMOF films that were observed (Figure 3).  Studies are underway to try to distinguish between these different possibilities to understand the discrepancy in the polyMOF stoichiometries.
MOF 1 is reported to have the potential for the capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from flue gases due to its high selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ vs $\Nu_{2}$ .23 Because polyMOFs can adopt the porosity from the parent MOFs, $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption were examined to evaluate the porosity of the polyMOFs prepared here.  The solventexchanged polyMOF samples were activated at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 10 h under vacuum.  As shown in Figure 7a, $Z\mathbf{n}$ -pbdc-8a(bpy), - 9a(bpy), and -12a(bpy) exhibited type II $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms, which are indicative of nonporous or macroporous adsorbents.25,26  Macroporosity could originate from the packing of the small polyMOF particles (Figure $3{\mathrm{.}}$ ). No BET surface areas were calculated based on $\Nu_{2}$ sorption due to very low $\Nu_{2}$ uptake in the low pressure region of the isotherm.  In contrast, Zn-pbdc-8a(bpy), -9a(bpy), and - 12a(bpy) were able to absorb $91\pm5$ , $86{\pm}3$ , and $75{\pm}5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ respectively, at 195 K and 1 bar (Figure 7b).  A $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $\mathrm{156~cm^{3}/g}$ at 195 K and 1 bar was reported for the original MOF ${\mathbf{1}},^{23}$ and we determined a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $160{\pm}6~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF $\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathbf{I}^{'}}$ prepared from BA-bdc (Figure 7b).
As shown in Figure 7c, for MOF $\scriptstyle2$ type polyMOFs, Znpbdc-7a(bpe), -8a(bpe), and -9a(bpe) demonstrated no detectable sorption of $\Nu_{2}$ at $_{77}\mathrm{~K~}$ in the low pressure region, while $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-10a(bpe), -11a(bpe), and -12a(bpe) exhibited type II $\Nu_{2}$ isotherms, again perhaps due to macrovoids between adjacent small particles (particle size $<2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\mathrm{,\}$ ).  For $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption, $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-7a(bpe), -8a(bpe), -9a(bpe), -10a(bpe) -11a(bpe), and -12a(bpe) possess $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of $72{\pm}2$ , $97^{\pm}5$ , $80{\pm}4$ , $140{\pm}5$ , $106{\pm}5$ , and $105{\pm}6~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively, at 195 K and 1 bar (Figure 7d).  In contrast, the parent MOF $\scriptstyle2$ activated under the same conditions as $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-xa(bpe) possessed a relatively low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity of $45{\pm}5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at 195 K and 1 bar.  PXRD revealed that MOF 2 decomposed after activation indicated by the complete disappearance of PXRD peaks after activation (Figure $\mathrm{S}_{30}\mathrm{\backslash}$ ).  Decreasing the activation temperature to room temperature resulted in an increase of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake to $72{\pm}6~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ for MOF $\scriptstyle2$ (Figure 7d). However, PXRD analysis revealed that MOF 2 transformed into an unknown crystalline phase after activation even at room temperature (Figure $\mathrm{S}_{30}$ ).
The observed selective adsorption of polyMOFs can be attributed to a kinetic sieving effect, where the small windows limit the diffusion of larger ${\bf N}_{2}$ molecules $(_{3.64}\mathrm{~\AA})$ into pores resulting in reduced adsorption, while smaller CO2 molecules $(3{\cdot}30\mathrm{\AA})$ are allowed to enter into the pores of the polyMOFs.27  This has been observed in several MOFs, which demonstrate selective adsorption for CO2 over other gases.28-31  Attempts to quantitatively evaluate the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ separation performance at 273 K and 298 K for these polyMOFs were not successful because $\Nu_{2}$ sorption tests were unable to produce smooth isotherm curves due to the extremely low $\Nu_{2}$ uptake (e.g. $\scriptstyle<1\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}/{\mathrm{g}}$ at 1 bar and 298K) (Figure $S_{31}$ ).  Methane uptake was also quite low in these polyMOFs (Figure $\displaystyle\mathbf{S}_{3}\mathbf{i}_{\mathrm{.}}$ ).  However, these polyMOFs can absorb significant amounts $\left(\mathbf{11-26\cm^{3}/g}\right)$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at 1 bar and 298 K (or 273 K) (Figure S31a, 31b).  The zero-coverage isosteric heat $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption for polyMOFs range from 24.9 to $30.9~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (Figure 31c), which are higher than other reported polyMOFs ( $\mathrm{Zn}$ -bpdc-7a and Zn-bpdc-8a)11 and some common MOFs such as IRMOF-1, IRMOF- $^{3}$ , and UMCM-1.32  The conclusion from these measurements is that all tested polyMOFs demonstrate relatively high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption but very low $\Nu_{2}$ sorption, making them promising materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\Nu_{2}$ separation, such as those sought in flue gas applications.
