Although MOF 1 possesses ultrahigh selectivity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ vs $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ vs $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , its instability toward water is limiting for real-world applications.23  Fischer and co-workers reported that MOF 1 transformed to an unknown phase after exposure to a water-saturated atmosphere.23  Along the same line, we observed that MOF $\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathbf{I}^{'}}$ transformed to a new phase after immersion in water for 1 day at room temperature (Figure $S_{32}\mathrm{\overline{{\Omega}}}$ ).  Similarly, PXRD revealed that MOF 2 was moisture sensitive and its structure decomposed upon exposure to atmospheric moisture or water for 1 day (Figure $S_{27}$ ).  As stated earlier, polyMOF variants are expected to have improved water stability compared with their parent MOFs because of polymer hydrophobicity.11  Contact angle measurements of polymer ligands with water (Table 1) showed that pbdc-9a, -10a, -11a, and -12a were hydrophobic with contact angles of $\mathbf{11}3^{\pm2^{\circ}}$ , $\mathbf{\Pi}_{\mathbf{u0\pm1}}\circ$ , $\mathbf{114}{\pm}\mathbf{1}^{\circ}$ , and $120{\pm}2^{\circ}$ , respectively.  Contact angles of the resulting polyMOF samples were also measured to determine if they inherited hydrophobicity from the polymer ligands.  Surprisingly, Znpbdc-8a(bpy), Zn-pbdc-9a(bpy), Zn-pbdc-7a(bpe), $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc8a(bpe), and Zn-pbdc-9a(bpe) were all relatively hydrophilic, while $Z\mathbf{n}$ -pbdc-12a(bpy), Zn-pbdc-10a(bpe), Zn-pbdc-11a(bpe) and Zn-pbdc-12a(bpe) were hydrophobic with contact angles of $\mathbf{\!11\pm1}^{\circ}$ , $\mathbf{114}{\pm}\mathbf{1}^{\circ}$ , $\mathbf{115^{\pm1}}^{\circ}$ , and $\bf{119}\pm\bf{1}^{\circ}$ , respectively.  These findings may best be attributed to the competition between the hydrophobic (e.g. polymer ligand) and hydrophilic (SBU) components of the polyMOFs.
Hydrophobicity alone does not ensure water-stability; therefore, PXRD patterns, SEM, and gas sorption experiments were performed for all polyMOFs after exposure to water.33  PolyMOF samples were immersed in water at room temperature or boiling for one day.  Water-exposed samples were solvent-exchanged with methanol for 5 days and then activated at 130 $^{\circ}C$ for 10 h under vacuum.  Except for Zn-pbdc-7a(bpe), which lost crystallinity after watertreatment, all other polyMOFs retain their crystallinity after water treatment (both room temperature and boiling) as confirmed by PXRD (Figure $S_{33}–S_{41})$ ).  SEM data revealed all water-treated polyMOFs retained their original morphology, suggesting there was no dissolution or degradation of the original phase (Figure $S_{42-S50}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ).  As summarized in Table 2, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption data revealed that Zn-pbdc-11a(bpe), Zn-pbdc12a(bpe), and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-12a(bpy) exhibited very similar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake values before and after water treatment (Figure $S51\mathrm{-}$ $\mathbf{S}_{53})$ , while the other polyMOF materials lost some porosity after exposure to water (Figure ${\cal S}54^{-{\cal S}59},$ ).  Overall, Zn-pbdc12a(bpy), $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-11a(bpe), and $Z\mathbf{n}$ -pbdc-12a(bpe) show exceptional water stability, while the remaining polyMOFs show significantly improved water resistance compared to their parent materials.
Because water vapor is a major component of industrial flue gas $(\sim\mathrm{1o\%})$ , water vapor treatment was also tested to evaluate the stability of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -pbdc-12a(bpy), Zn-pbdc-11a(bpe), and $Z\mathbf{n}$ -pbdc-12a(bpe), which showed the highest stability to liquid water.  PXRD, SEM, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption data were obtained for these polyMOFs exposed to $90\%$ relative humidity at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 7 days.  Literature reports indicate that many MOFs, such as HKUST-1 and ${\bf M O F-74}$ , are not stable under these conditions.34  Both PXRD and SEM data revealed $Z\mathbf{n}$ -pbdc-12a(bpy), Zn-pbdc-11a(bpe), and $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-12a(bpe) retained their crystallinity and original morphology (Figures $S_{37}$ , $S_{3}8$ , S41, S46, S47, S50) under the aforementioned conditions. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption data revealed $Z\mathrm{n}$ -pbdc-11a(bpe) lost some capacity, giving a lower uptake of $75~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at 195 K (Figure $\mathbf{S51}_{\cdot}\mathbf{\dot{\Omega}}$ ).  Zn-pbdc-12a(bpe) and Zn-pbdc-12a(bpy) exhibited $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptakes of 98 and $78\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}.$ , respectively—very close to their uptake values (Table 2) prior to water vapor treatment (Figure $\mathbf{S}52,\ \mathbf{S}53)$ .  For comparison, MOF $\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathbf{I}^{'}}$ and MOF 2 were treated under the same conditions as the aforementioned polyMOFs.  PXRD revealed that MOF $\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathbf{I}^{'}}$ retained crystallinity, while MOF 2 did not, further demonstrating the ability of polyMOFs to form analogues of MOFs that are sensitive to either water liquid or vapor.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, we report a mixed ligand strategy, which uses both polymer ligands and simple pillaring pyridine linkers, to prepare new polyMOFs.  We also demonstrate that polyMOFs with other metal ions, such as $\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}$ can be prepared, strongly suggesting that our polyMOF approach can be generalized to other metal ions.  This finding demonstrates that polyMOFs are compatible with a variety of MOF architectures besides that of the well-studied IRMOF-1. Gas sorption studies revealed that these new materials demonstrated relatively high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption, but very low $\Nu_{2}$ uptake, making them promising materials to separate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from industrial flue gases.  Although the parent MOFs are generally unstable to water, the polyMOFs demonstrated excellent water stability.  The enhanced water stability is attributed to the incorporation of the hydrophobic polymer ligands, as well as the cross-linking of the MOF lattice by the polymer chains.  These findings suggest that polyMOFs may be a general strategy for enabling or improving existing MOFs that need to be made more durable for real-world applications.
